Back to Articles A Closer Look at Phonon TTS. Launching High-Quality On-Device Speech That Tops the Benchmarks. Published November 5, 2026 Update on GitHub Upvote 17 +11 Olesia Krizhevich olesiagradium Follow gradium Laurent Mazare lmz Follow gradium Eric Bezzam bezzam Follow Eustache Le Bihan eustlb Follow Two weeks ago, we opened up Phonon, our small text-to-speech model that runs on your own device. It needs no server and no network, and what you type stays with you. We've been very happy with the reception so far. So here's the story of the launch: why we built Phonon, what it can do, the demos that came with it, and how it did on Hugging Face's Open TTS Leaderboard. Why a voice should live on your device Most voice AI lives in the cloud: every sentence makes a round trip to a server. Phonon runs on your own device instead. Your text stays there and the voice works without a network. We’ve also opened the weights. Phonon 31m is Apache 2.0, so you can use it for free, even in a commercial product. The other models are CC BY-NC, so they’re free for non-commercial projects. If you’re building something commercial with those, pricing is based on your number of users, not how much speech you generate. Getting a good voice to run on a small device has always been hard. You could make the model small, but then it didn’t sound great. Or you could make it sound great, but then it wouldn’t fit. We addressed both. We announced Phonon in April and spent the next six months building on it with partners and early users. Their feedback helped us to shape this release. The models got smaller, and we added more languages and support for custom voices through voice cloning and voice design. Phonon is based on the Pocket TTS lineage and as such uses flow matching to generate the audio which is very different from the modeling techniques used in other TTS systems. This is what allowed us to bring this level of quality to such a small model but it created new challenges while training the models. The training was initially numerically unstable and required several tricks to produce quality results on all five languages. What we released Phonon comes in two sizes, at about 65 MB and 75 MB. Together they speak English, French, German, Spanish and Portuguese. There are more than 100 built-in voices, and you can create your own in Gradium Studio . Phonon also handles the important things that most other TTS systems often stumble on, like phone numbers, dates and codes. It runs on phones, laptops, single-board computers and in a browser tab, so you can hear it right now . Phonon 31m speaks English and is Apache 2.0, so you can build on it freely, including for commercial products. Phonon 40m covers all five languages under a non-commercial license. Demos and first projects The launch came with demos from friends and from our own team. On an Apple Watch with Wi-Fi off, Phonon talks you through a coffee brew step by step. On an iPhone with no signal, it counts your reps and keeps you going. A robot called Lamp, from Autonomous, wears a sorting hat and talks in character. In a browser, an offline dino runner becomes a radio show hosted by Rex. There's also a sci-fi game voiced on the player's own machine, and Proust read aloud page by page. The Hugging Face team built the Transformers integration, so developers could use Phonon in Transformers from day one. Developers have started building on it too. An ExecuTorch conversion runs Phonon on a visitor's GPU in the browser, Micdrop brings it to Node.js apps, and Spaces like smallest-s2s build on it. Phonon was downloaded more than 200,000 times in its first two weeks, and it’s still trending on Hugging Face. How Phonon did on the Hugging Face Open TTS Leaderboard The Open TTS Leaderboard is a very new project from Hugging Face, and we love it. With more than 9,000 TTS models on the Hugging Face Hub , the leaderboard gives developers one place to compare open models and hear them side by side. Across 23 languages, models are evaluated on intelligibility ( word error rate of the outputs ), speed ( inverse real-time factor and time-to-first audio ), and naturalness ( UTMOS for English and KOMOS for Korean ). Fixed-voice and voice cloning are evaluated, with an additional speaker similarity metric for the latter. The leaderboard also has a Listen tab, where you can conveniently compare TTS outputs yourself and vote for the ones you like the most. On intelligibility, Phonon ranks first in German and Spanish , and in the top three in every language we evaluated, next to models up to 100 times its size. Speed is where we see Phonon truly shine, as the models top the CPU streaming benchmark on time-to-first audio. Inference We built a new inference framework, ptts , specifically for Phonon. The goal of ptts is to get the best performance on a wide variety of devices and as such supports more than 10 backends: CPU (Intel, Arm, WebAssembly), GPU (CUDA, Vulkan, Metal, WebGPU), and NPU (Apple Core ML, Snapdragon®). Phonon is also available in 🤗 Transformers, from v5.21, as its reference PyTorch implementation. Where ptts runs a single stream on device, Transformers targets GPU throughput: batched generation, full-graph torch.compile , e.g., for synthesizing speech datasets at scale. Being native PyTorch, it is a base to build upon, whether to integrate Phonon into your own stack or to study the model itself. It belongs to the Pocket TTS lineage, so we first added Pocket TTS to Transformers. Phonon sits on top as a modular model: about 200 lines describing only what differs. Phonon generates audio latents autoregressively: each step outputs a latent that is fed back as the next input, and the training loss is computed on them. Latents are the model's inputs, outputs and targets, as text tokens are for a language model, so the codec mapping audio to latents plays the role of a tokenizer. Transformers ships it as such: an audio tokenizer , held by the processor next to the text tokenizer: from transformers import AutoProcessor, GradiumPhononTTSForConditionalGeneration model_id = "gradium/phonon-31m-en" processor = AutoProcessor.from_pretrained(model_id, device_map= "auto" )
model = GradiumPhononTTSForConditionalGeneration.from_pretrained(model_id, device_map= "auto" ) print ( f" {model.num_parameters():,} " ) # 30,788,354 print ( f" {processor.audio_tokenizer.num_parameters():,} " ) # 10,305,313 A voice is not a recording but a tensor of speaker latents. Phonon never encodes audio directly with its audio tokenizer, which ships decoder-only, and voices come precomputed: gradium/phonon-voices . from huggingface_hub import hf_hub_download from safetensors.torch import load_file path = hf_hub_download( "gradium/phonon-voices" , "voices/LMDIYE0Oa377Qh6Z.safetensors" )
voice = load_file(path)[ "speaker_wavs" ][ 0 ].T # (num_frames, 512) The prompt layout (voice positions, text, then a token opening the audio) ships with the checkpoint as a chat template, as for other multimodal models in Transformers. You write a standard conversation, with an empty audio item where the voice goes, and your code stays the same across checkpoints. The processor then expands the voice placeholder to one position per frame, normalizes and tokenizes the text, and batches the inputs: conversation = [ { "role" : "user" , "content" : [{ "type" : "audio" }, { "type" : "text" , "text" : "Hello from Phonon." }]}
]
inputs = processor.apply_chat_template( conversation, tokenize= True , return_dict= True , add_generation_prompt= True , processor_kwargs={ "audio_prompt_latents" : [voice]},
).to(model.device) # prompt: <|audio_prompt|> × num_frames, "Hello from Phonon.", <|audio_bos|> generate returns latents, which the processor decodes to audio: outputs = model.generate(**inputs) # audio_latents: (batch_size, num_frames, 32) audio = processor.decode(outputs.audio_latents, outputs.audio_latents_mask)
processor.save_audio(audio, "phonon.wav" ) See the docs 🤗 for more!

Agent Toolkit for AWS Help AI coding agents build, deploy, and manage applications on AWS. The Agent Toolkit for AWS gives AI coding agents the tools, knowledge, and guardrails they need to work with AWS services. It works with the coding agents developers already use — including Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Devin, Kiro, and fx. Quick start AWS CLI Use the Agent Toolkit directly from your terminal with the AWS CLI: aws configure agent-toolkit See the AWS CLI integration guide for setup, configuration, and usage instructions. Claude Code The plugins are available on the official Anthropic marketplace ( claude-plugins-official ) which is added to your Claude Code installation by default.
Use the following commands to install supported plugins from the toolkit: For aws-core that covers service selection, CDK/CloudFormation, serverless, containers, storage, observability, billing, SDK usage, and deployment: /plugin install aws-core@claude-plugins-official Tip: If you get Plugin not found , update your local marketplace index first: /plugin marketplace update claude-plugins-official For aws-agents that covers building AI agents on AWS with Amazon Bedrock and AgentCore: /plugin install aws-agents@claude-plugins-official For aws-data-analytics that covers data lake, analytics, and ETL workflows with S3 Tables, AWS Glue, and Athena: /plugin install aws-data-analytics@claude-plugins-official For aws-agents-for-devsecops used to investigate incidents, review code and execute UAT for release readiness, scan code for vulnerabilities, and run penetration tests with AWS DevOps Agent and AWS Security Agent. /plugin marketplace add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws
/plugin install aws-agents-for-devsecops
/reload-plugins

# Or from Claude's official marketplace:
/plugin install aws-agents-for-devsecops@claude-plugins-official
/reload-plugins

# Setup:
/aws-agents-for-devsecops:setup For aws-startup-advisor that covers startup-focused architecture, cost, security, and AWS Activate guidance, plus migrations to AWS from Azure, GCP, Heroku, and AI stacks such as OpenAI and Gemini, install from this repository's marketplace: /plugin marketplace add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws
/plugin install aws-startup-advisor@agent-toolkit-for-aws
/reload-plugins Codex In your terminal: codex plugin marketplace add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws Then launch Codex and run /plugins to browse and install the aws-core plugin. Cursor Add this repository as a team marketplace from Settings → Plugins → Team Marketplaces → Add Marketplace → Import from Repo , pointing it at aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws . Cursor indexes the plugins listed in .cursor-plugin/marketplace.json on import. Then open the Plugins panel and install the aws-core plugin (start here), or aws-agents and aws-data-analytics as needed. Each plugin bundles the AWS MCP Server configuration and agent skills. Devin In your terminal: devin plugins install aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws#plugins/aws-core This requires devin auth login and adds the plugin to your personal plugins, so it also loads in Devin cloud sessions and Devin Desktop. Add --local to install on this machine only. To install a different plugin, replace aws-core with its name from Plugins , such as aws-agents or aws-data-analytics . In the Devin web app, choose Customize → Plugins → Add plugin → From repository , enter aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws , and set the subdirectory to plugins/aws-core (or another plugin's directory). Personal plugins added there are also available in the Devin CLI and Devin Desktop. Kiro Kiro setup has two independent parts: the AWS MCP Server (for runtime AWS API access and documentation search) and local skills (for task-specific agent guidance). They complement each other but work independently — skills don't require the MCP server, and the MCP server doesn't serve locally-installed skills. 1. Add the AWS MCP Server to your Kiro MCP configuration ( .kiro/settings/mcp.json ): { "mcpServers" : { "aws" : { "command" : " uvx " , "args" : [ " mcp-proxy-for-aws-cli@latest " , " https://aws-mcp.us-east-1.api.aws/mcp " , " --metadata " , " AWS_REGION=us-west-2 " ]
    }
  }
} The MCP server gives your agent access to AWS APIs, sandboxed script execution, and real-time documentation search. 2. Install skills from this repository: npx skills add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/skills This installs skill files to ~/.kiro/skills/ (global) or .kiro/skills/ (project-level). Each skill is a directory containing a SKILL.md file and optionally a references/ subdirectory with additional context the agent reads from the local filesystem when needed. Kiro discovers installed skills automatically and activates them on demand when a task matches. Prerequisites: You need uv installed. An AWS account with credentials configured locally is required for API calls and script execution, but not for documentation search or skill discovery. See the user guide for detailed setup instructions. fx fx setup has the same two independent parts as Kiro: the AWS MCP Server (for runtime AWS API access and documentation search) and local skills (for task-specific agent guidance). Skills don't require the MCP server, and the MCP server doesn't serve locally-installed skills. 1. Add the AWS MCP Server from your terminal. fx stores MCP servers in ~/.fx/mcp.json , so the server is available in every project: fx mcp add aws uvx mcp-proxy-for-aws-cli@latest https://aws-mcp.us-east-1.api.aws/mcp --metadata AWS_REGION=us-west-2 Or add it to ~/.fx/mcp.json by hand. fx uses the mcp key rather than mcpServers , type to select the transport, and a single command array holding the executable and its arguments: { "mcp" : { "aws" : { "type" : " local " , "command" : [ " uvx " , " mcp-proxy-for-aws-cli@latest " , " https://aws-mcp.us-east-1.api.aws/mcp " , " --metadata " , " AWS_REGION=us-west-2 " ]
    }
  }
} If a session is already open when you edit the file, run /mcp reload to pick up the change. The MCP server gives your agent access to AWS APIs, sandboxed script execution, and real-time documentation search. 2. Install skills from this repository: npx skills add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/skills -a fx This installs skill files to .fx/skills/ (project-level); add -g to install to ~/.fx/skills/ (global). fx also discovers skills already installed for other agents in .agents/skills/ , .claude/skills/ , and .codex/skills/ , so a project set up for Codex or Claude Code needs no second copy. Each skill is a directory containing a SKILL.md file and optionally a references/ subdirectory with additional context the agent reads from the local filesystem when needed. fx reads skill metadata at startup and loads a skill's instructions when you or the agent invokes it; type $ in the composer or run /skills to browse what's installed. Prerequisites: You need uv installed. An AWS account with credentials configured locally is required for API calls and script execution, but not for documentation search or skill discovery. See the user guide for detailed setup instructions. Other agents See the AWS MCP Server getting started guide for instructions on configuring the AWS MCP Server with your agent. Then install skills from this repository: npx skills add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/skills Prerequisites: You need uv installed. An AWS account with credentials configured locally is required for API calls and script execution, but not for documentation search or skill discovery. See the user guide for detailed setup instructions. What's included Plugins Plugins bundle the AWS MCP Server configuration and agent skills into a single install for your coding agent. Plugin Description aws-core Core AWS skills and MCP Server configuration. Covers service selection, CDK/CloudFormation, serverless, containers, storage, observability, billing, SDK usage, and deployment. Start here. aws-agents Skills for building AI agents on AWS with Amazon Bedrock and AgentCore.

Figma Console MCP Server Your design system as an API. A design-system-focused Model Context Protocol server for Figma. It works in both directions (Figma ⇄ code), writes to Figma , and runs deterministic checks for design-code parity, accessibility, and design-system health. It hands the AI exact tokens, variants, bindings, and states as structured data rather than prescribing a framework or house style, so generated code follows your team's own stack and conventions. 🆕 Shaders, grid and auto layout, OKLCH and P3 color, text wrap and variable fonts (latest v1.46.0): apply shaders and set their properties by name ( figma_apply_shader , figma_list_shaders ), set auto layout or grid layout in one call with CSS-style grid tracks ( figma_set_layout ), use OKLCH, Display P3 and other CSS colors on fills and strokes, and set text wrap (balance, pretty) and variable-font axes with figma_set_text (v1.46.0). v1.45 added Motion: animate layers with presets, custom animation styles or keyframes bound to Timing and Easing tokens, export video, and find code layers. See what's new → What is this? Figma Console MCP connects AI assistants (like Claude) to Figma, enabling: 🎨 Design system extraction - Pull variables, components, and styles 🧬 Codebase → design system extraction - Scan a production codebase, mine its de-facto styling into DTCG tokens with per-token provenance, scaffold a design-system package with a wired Storybook workshop, and gate it with deterministic fidelity evals — then round-trip the tokens into Figma variables (Local Mode) 🔁 Bidirectional token sync - Export Figma variables to DTCG JSON (legacy or 2025.10 dialect) + 9 more formats; push code-side edits back to Figma with full apply — creates, renames, alias re-targeting, and replace-gated deletes. Replaces Style Dictionary and Tokens Studio's export pipeline. 📸 Visual debugging - Take screenshots for context ✏️ Design creation - Create UI components, frames, and layouts directly in Figma — including one-call component-set creation from a variant axes matrix 🔧 Variable management - Create, update, rename, and delete design tokens 🕰 Version history & time-series awareness - List versions, diff snapshots, generate markdown changelogs, trace property/variant introduction via binary-search blame, and fold per-component design + git history into generated component docs ⚡ Real-time monitoring - Watch console logs from the Desktop Bridge plugin 📌 FigJam boards - Create stickies, flowcharts, tables, and code blocks on collaborative boards 🎞️ Slides presentations - Build and manage Figma Slides decks programmatically ♿ Accessibility scanning - 14 WCAG design checks with conformance level tagging, component scorecards, axe-core code scanning, design-to-code parity 🔍 Design-code parity & component docs - Deterministic comparison of a Figma component with its code (visual, spacing, typography, tokens, component API, accessibility), and generated component documentation with per-variant tokens and an optional design + git changelog 📋 Design-system health audits - Lighthouse-style scored audit across naming, tokens, component metadata, accessibility, consistency, and coverage — available as a plain tool for any MCP client (with per-finding auto-fix guidance) and as a visual dashboard app 🛡 Cross-MCP identity - Every tool response carries _mcp: "figma-console-mcp" and errors are prefixed [figma-console-mcp] so attribution stays unambiguous in agents running multiple Figma MCPs ☁️ Cloud Write Relay - Web AI clients (Claude.ai, v0, Replit) can design in Figma via cloud pairing 🔄 Four ways to connect - Remote SSE, Cloud Mode, NPX, or Local Git ⚡ Quick Start Choose Your Setup First, decide what you want to do: I want to... Setup Method Time Create and modify designs with AI NPX Setup (Recommended) ~10 min Design from the web (Claude.ai, v0, Replit, Lovable) Cloud Mode ~5 min Contribute to the project Local Git Setup ~15 min Just explore my design data (read-only) Remote SSE ~2 min ⚠️ Important: Capability Differences Capability NPX / Local Git Cloud Mode Remote SSE Read design data ✅ ✅ ✅ Create components & frames ✅ ✅ ❌ Edit existing designs ✅ ✅ ❌ Manage design tokens/variables ✅ ✅ ❌ FigJam boards (stickies, flowcharts) ✅ ✅ ❌ Real-time monitoring (console, selection) ✅ ❌ ❌ Codebase → design system extraction ( figma_ds_* ) ✅ ❌ ❌ Desktop Bridge plugin ✅ ✅ ❌ Requires Node.js Yes No No Total tools available 130 105 after pairing Read-only subset Bottom line: Remote SSE is read-only until you pair the plugin. Cloud Mode unlocks write access (105 tools) from web AI clients without Node.js. NPX/Local Git gives the full 130 tools with real-time monitoring. 🚀 NPX Setup (Recommended) Best for: Designers who want full AI-assisted design capabilities. What you get: All 130 tools including design creation, variable management, and component instantiation. Prerequisites Node.js 18+ — Check with node --version ( Download ) Figma Desktop installed (not just the web app) An MCP client (Claude Code, Cursor, Windsurf, Claude Desktop, etc.) Step 1: Get Your Figma Token Go to Manage personal access tokens in Figma Help Follow the steps to create a new personal access token Enter description: Figma Console MCP Set scopes (all Read unless noted): File content , File versions , Library content , Library assets , Comments (Read and write), Current user , and Variables (Enterprise only, optional). See why each is needed Copy the token — you won't see it again! (starts with figd_ ) Step 2: Configure Your MCP Client Claude Code (CLI): claude mcp add figma-console -s user -e FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN=figd_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE -e ENABLE_MCP_APPS=true -- npx -y figma-console-mcp@latest OpenAI Codex (CLI): codex mcp add figma-console --env FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN=figd_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE --env ENABLE_MCP_APPS=true -- npx -y figma-console-mcp@latest Then set startup_timeout_sec = 60 and tool_timeout_sec = 300 for it in ~/.codex/config.toml (Codex's 10 s / 60 s defaults are too short). The Codex app and IDE extension share this config. Full Codex setup → Gemini CLI: gemini mcp add -s user -e FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN=figd_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE -e ENABLE_MCP_APPS=true figma-console npx -y figma-console-mcp@latest Google Antigravity (IDE, CLI, app): add the JSON below to ~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json , plus "timeoutSeconds": 300 in the server entry. Full Antigravity setup → Cursor / Windsurf / Claude Desktop: Add to your MCP config file (see Where to find your config file below): { "mcpServers" : { "figma-console" : { "command" : " npx " , "args" : [ " -y " , " figma-console-mcp@latest " ], "env" : { "FIGMA_ACCESS_TOKEN" : " figd_YOUR_TOKEN_HERE " , "ENABLE_MCP_APPS" : " true " }
    }
  }
} 📂 Where to Find Your Config File If you're not sure where to put the JSON configuration above, here's where each app stores its MCP config: App macOS Windows Claude Desktop ~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json %APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json Claude Code (CLI) ~/.claude.json %USERPROFILE%\.claude.json Cursor ~/.cursor/mcp.json %USERPROFILE%\.cursor\mcp.json Windsurf ~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json %USERPROFILE%\.codeium\windsurf\mcp_config.json OpenAI Codex (app, CLI, IDE) ~/.codex/config.toml (TOML, see format ) %USERPROFILE%\.codex\config.toml Gemini CLI ~/.gemini/settings.json %USERPROFILE%\.gemini\settings.json Google Antigravity (IDE, CLI, app) ~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json %USERPROFILE%\.gemini\config\mcp_config.json Tip for designers: The ~ symbol means your home folder . On macOS, that's /Users/YourName/ . On Windows, it's C:\Users\YourName\ . You can open these files in any text editor — even TextEdit or Notepad. Can't find the file? If it doesn't exist yet, create it. The app will pick it up on its next restart. Make sure the entire file is valid JSON (watch for missing commas or brackets). Claude Code users: You can skip manual editing entirely. Just run the claude mcp add command above and it handles everything for you.
